            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"Abundance, prosperity, and divine provision, frequently paired with honey to signify the richness of the land.\", \"primary_basis\": [\"Job 20:17\", \"Isaiah 7:22\"], \"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"Subsistence, devastation, or the simplicity of a peasant diet, often used Christologically to denote the genuine humanity and humble upbringing of the incarnate Christ.\", \"basis\": [\"Isaiah 7:15\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Lavish hospitality, honor, and richness offered to esteemed guests.\", \"basis\": [\"Genesis 18:8\", \"Judges 5:25\"], \"prominence\": \"minority\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Instigation, agitation, or the natural consequence of pressure through the metaphor of churning.\", \"basis\": [\"Proverbs 30:33\"], \"prominence\": \"rare\"}], \"has_settled_consensus\": false, \"confidence\": \"high\", \"notes\": \"The symbolism of butter or curds is heavily context-dependent and debated, particularly in Isaiah 7. It can paradoxically represent both the extreme abundance of a blessed land and the forced subsistence diet of a devastated people living off uncultivated pastures. In Patristic interpretation, it frequently symbolized the true physical incarnation and human infancy of Christ.\"}",
